{
  "image_name": "88d5b0d2765ecd9726d92bc08fe33459.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a conventional perovskite proton conductor with a hydrogenated samarium nickel oxide material, H-SNO, and explains why the latter conducts protons while suppressing electronic leakage. In the top schematics, proton uptake is shown as water or hydrogen introducing H+ into an oxide lattice. In the conventional perovskite, proton incorporation is associated with oxygen vacancies and aliovalent dopants such as Y3+ in a Zr4+ framework. In H-SNO, by contrast, H+ appears to be incorporated in a nickel oxide lattice containing Ni2+/Ni3+ and Sm3+, with nearby oxygen carrying partial negative charge.\n\nThe lower diagram shows the device concept: platinum electrodes contact an H-SNO layer, hydrogen is oxidized at one side to produce protons and electrons, the protons move through H-SNO, and oxygen is reduced at the other side to form water. The electrons travel through the external circuit, lighting a bulb.\n\nThe right-side band sketches emphasize the key advantage of H-SNO: electron doping changes the nickel valence and electronic structure so that electronic transport is suppressed, unlike a more metallic state that would allow electron flow through the oxide. Overall, the figure presents H-SNO as a proton-conducting electrolyte with reduced electronic conductivity.",
